[{"id":1501,"slug":"dribbble-select-best-shots-2026","title":"Unfold sélectionné parmi les meilleurs projets de l’année sur Dribbble","excerpt":"Deux reconnaissances : notre travail figure dans la sélection annuelle des meilleurs projets Dribbble et dans le nouveau répertoire Dribbble Select des agences de branding.","body":"We don't put out a lot of announcements. When something is worth saying, we say it. This is worth saying.\n\nDribbble has selected Unfold for two recognitions this year: a feature in their annual **\"Best Shots of the Year\"** editorial curation, and a listing in the newly launched **Dribbble Select Branding Agencies** directory.\n\n![Unfold Branding Work](/images/case-studies/safepal-hero.png)\n\n## What Dribbble Select Is\n\n\"Best Shots of the Year\" is Dribbble's annual editorial — a hand-picked showcase of standout design work from established teams. It's not algorithmic. It's a curation by the Dribbble team of the work they consider the best of the year.\n\nThe Dribbble Select Branding Agencies directory is a separate, specialized space for discovering agencies equipped for complex, large-scale creative projects. It's a short list. We're on it.\n\n## What It Means for Us\n\nWe're proud of this. Not because external validation is the point — the work is the point — but because the work speaks for itself here. The projects that got us here weren't simple. Rebranding a streaming platform. Building a visual identity for a crypto ecosystem at scale. Designing for a sport's most recognizable athlete. These are complex, high-stakes briefs that demand more than good taste.\n\nGetting named to a directory built specifically for teams that handle that kind of work feels right.\n\n## What It Means for You\n\nIf you're evaluating design partners for something that isn't simple — a brand launch, a platform rebrand, a product that needs to look like it belongs at the top of its category — this is context worth having.\n\nWe're the agency Dribbble points to when someone asks who handles the complex stuff.\n\n[Check it out](https://dribbble.com/branding-agency)","category":"News","author":"Unfold Team","authorRole":null,"publishedAt":"March 25, 2026","readingTime":"2 min read","heroImage":"/images/journal/dribbble-select-hero.png","featured":true,"displayOrder":0,"searchTags":["dribbble","award","recognition","branding","design agency","news","brand design","unfold"]},{"id":37,"slug":"logomark-layout-proportions","title":"Logomark à droite : ce que les designers doivent savoir sur la composition d’un logo","excerpt":"La plupart des chartes placent le symbole à gauche. Voici quand et pourquoi le positionner à droite du logotype.","body":"Most logo usage guides show the logomark on the left. It's the default. Open any brand book, and chances are the primary horizontal lockup has the symbol sitting neatly to the left of the wordmark. But what happens when you flip it?\n\n![Logo layout variations showing mark placement options](/images/journal/logo_mark_right_1.webp)\n\n## Why most logos place the symbol on the left\n\nThere's a practical reason for this convention. In left-to-right reading cultures, the eye enters a composition from the left. Placing the symbol there means it's the first thing seen — an anchor that establishes brand recognition before the name is even read.\n\n![Standard left-positioned logomark layout](/images/journal/logo_mark_right_3.webp)\n\nThis works well in most contexts: website headers, business cards, letterheads. The symbol leads, the name follows. It mirrors how we process information sequentially.\n\n## When the right side makes more sense\n\nBut not every context rewards convention. There are specific situations where placing the logomark on the right creates a stronger composition:\n\n**Navigation headers** — When a logo sits in the top-left corner of a website, the wordmark naturally aligns with the left edge. Moving the symbol to the right keeps it from colliding with the browser edge or navigation elements, giving the whole lockup more breathing room.\n\n![Right-positioned logomark in navigation context](/images/journal/logo_mark_right_2.webp)\n\n**Right-aligned layouts** — In editorial or asymmetric designs, a right-side logo can balance the composition. If your layout already has strong left-weighted content, a right-aligned logomark acts as a visual counterweight.\n\n**RTL (right-to-left) languages** — For brands operating in Arabic, Hebrew, or other RTL markets, flipping the logomark to the right side of the logotype respects the natural reading direction. This isn't just a design preference — it's a functional requirement.\n\n## The weight distribution problem\n\nHere's the real issue most designers miss: the visual weight of the logomark versus the logotype.\n\n![Visual weight comparison between logomark and logotype](/images/journal/logo_mark_right_4.webp)\n\nA dense, filled symbol carries more visual mass than lightweight typography. When that heavy element sits on the left, it creates a natural reading flow — heavy to light. Move it to the right, and you get the opposite: light to heavy. This can feel like the composition is falling off the edge.\n\nThe fix isn't to avoid right-placed symbols. It's to adjust the spacing. A logomark on the right typically needs slightly more padding between it and the wordmark to feel balanced.\n\n## How we handle it at Unfold\n\nAt Unfold, we usually develop both versions — symbol-left and symbol-right — during the brand identity phase. Each version gets tested in real contexts: headers, footers, social avatars, merchandise.\n\n![Unfold logo lockup variants](/images/journal/logo_mark_right_6.webp)\n\nWe've found that the right-placed symbol works best when:\n- The logomark is simple and geometric (low visual complexity)\n- The logotype has strong enough weight to lead the eye\n- The application context naturally anchors the left side\n\n![Real-world examples of right-positioned logomarks](/images/journal/logo_mark_right_5.webp)\n\n## The real answer\n\nThere's no universal rule that says the logomark must sit on the left. What matters is whether the placement serves the context. A good logo system includes multiple lockup variations — horizontal, vertical, symbol-only — and the designer's job is to define clear guidance on when to use each one.\n\n> Don't follow convention blindly. Follow the logic of the layout.\n\nIf placing the symbol on the right creates better balance, better hierarchy, or better integration with the surrounding design, then that's where it belongs.","category":"Identité de marque","author":"Benjamin Oberemok","authorRole":"Creative Director","publishedAt":"Aug 6, 2025","readingTime":"5 min read","heroImage":"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1000/1*0utFOh1o_yuJTk5dQkzIYw.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":1,"searchTags":null},{"id":38,"slug":"icon-styles-choosing-right-one","title":"Mélanger plusieurs styles d’icônes sur un site : bonne ou mauvaise idée ?","excerpt":"Peut-on mélanger des icônes filaires et pleines sur un même site ? Voici comment faire le bon choix.","body":"A client recently asked us to \"spice up\" their icon set by mixing outline and filled styles on the same page. Their reasoning was simple: variety makes things more interesting. That reasoning was wrong.\n\n## The basics: what are icon styles?\n\nBefore we get into mixing, let's define the playing field. Icon styles generally fall into a few categories:\n\n![Different icon style categories](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*z3FaLCuEyiG8WxPtjC5l2Q.png)\n\n**Outline (linear)** — Icons drawn with strokes only. Clean, modern, minimal. Lower visual weight.\n\n**Filled (solid)** — Icons using solid shapes. Higher visual weight, stronger presence. Easier to recognize at small sizes.\n\n**Duotone** — Two-tone fills, often using opacity to create depth. A middle ground between outline and filled.\n\n**Hand-drawn / illustrative** — Custom icons with personality. Great for branding, less practical for functional UI.\n\n## Why mixing feels wrong\n\nEach icon style carries a specific visual weight and personality. Outline icons feel lighter, more delicate. Filled icons feel heavier, more assertive. When you mix them arbitrarily, you create an inconsistency that the eye picks up immediately — even if the viewer can't articulate why something feels off.\n\n![Inconsistent icon styles on a webpage](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*NyVcDaT_k1h0hD2fORCDZQ.png)\n\nThis is the same reason you wouldn't mix serif and sans-serif body text within the same paragraph. The contrast isn't purposeful — it's accidental.\n\n## When mixing actually works\n\nThat said, there are legitimate cases where mixing icon styles is intentional and effective:\n\n**State differentiation** — Using outline icons for inactive states and filled icons for active states. This is a well-established pattern in navigation (think iOS tab bars). The style change communicates function, not decoration.\n\n![Active vs inactive icon states](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*bPiVG_GYnRMXLbAbfkRuvA.png)\n\n**Hierarchy** — Filled icons for primary actions, outline for secondary. This creates a visual hierarchy that guides attention. The filled icon carries more weight, drawing the eye first.\n\n**Branding vs. UI** — Using illustrative icons for marketing sections and functional icons for the interface. These live in different contexts, so the contrast is expected and natural.\n\n## The rules we follow at Unfold\n\nWhen we design icon systems for clients, we follow a few principles:\n\n1. **One style per context.** Within a single section, toolbar, or navigation, use one style. Don't mix.\n\n2. **Style changes must communicate something.** If you switch from outline to filled, that switch should mean something (active state, emphasis, category change).\n\n3. **Maintain consistent stroke weight.** If you're using outline icons, they should all share the same stroke width. A 1.5px icon next to a 2px icon creates subtle but real dissonance.\n\n4. **Test at actual size.** An outline icon that looks great at 48px might disappear at 16px. Always verify your icons at their smallest intended display size.\n\n![Icon sizing and stroke weight comparison](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*OBhb2fTXIg5oSlL-fILEuw.png)\n\n## The practical answer\n\nCan you mix icon styles on a website? Yes — but only when the mixing is systematic and meaningful. Random mixing creates visual noise. Strategic mixing creates hierarchy and clarity.\n\n> Consistency isn't about using the same icon style everywhere. It's about making sure every style choice has a reason.\n\nThe safest approach: pick one primary icon style for your interface. Use a second style only when you need to communicate a specific state change or hierarchy shift. And if you're not sure whether mixing adds value, it probably doesn't.\n\n![Consistent icon system example](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*zNu03GqUE_4lDjwpZvTfDw.png)","category":"Design produit","author":"Benjamin Oberemok","authorRole":"Creative Director","publishedAt":"Aug 4, 2025","readingTime":"5 min read","heroImage":"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1000/1*FWav16ZKdbP-7nRUC9shsA.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":2,"searchTags":null},{"id":39,"slug":"responsive-logos-guide","title":"A step-by-step process for creating responsive logo designs","excerpt":"A responsive logo adapts to different sizes and contexts without losing its identity. Here's our step-by-step process for creating logo systems that work everywhere.","body":"A logo designed for a letterhead won't work as a 32px favicon. A detailed wordmark that shines on a billboard becomes illegible on a smartwatch. This isn't a new problem, but the number of contexts a logo needs to work in has grown dramatically.\n\n## What is a responsive logo?\n\nA responsive logo isn't a single design — it's a system of related versions that share the same DNA but adapt to different sizes and contexts.\n\n![Responsive logo size variations](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*AwzFHQmePrVGJnvuPkCpBQ.png)\n\nThink of it like responsive web design. The content doesn't change, but how it's presented shifts based on the available space. A responsive logo system typically includes:\n\n- **Full lockup** — Logomark + logotype, used when space allows\n- **Compact lockup** — Tighter spacing or stacked arrangement\n- **Logomark only** — The symbol alone, for small spaces\n- **Simplified logomark** — Reduced detail for very small sizes (favicons, app icons)\n\n## Step 1: Start with the full version\n\nAlways design the most detailed version first. This is your reference — the version that contains all the information about your brand's visual identity.\n\n![Full logo version as starting point](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*PaPo3AHOPgjlpzLj_pHj0g.png)\n\nDon't think about simplification yet. Establish the ideal version: the one that would go on a business card, a website header at full size, or a presentation slide.\n\n## Step 2: Identify the core elements\n\nWhat makes your logo recognizable? Strip away everything that doesn't contribute to instant recognition:\n\n- Is it the symbol? The letterform? A specific color?\n- What's the minimum you can show while keeping the brand identifiable?\n\nThis analysis determines how you'll reduce the logo across sizes.\n\n![Core element identification in logo design](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*f1JN6TW5s5Rb2IjDpCfU0Q.png)\n\n## Step 3: Define your breakpoints\n\nJust like CSS breakpoints, logo breakpoints are the thresholds where one version replaces another. These aren't arbitrary — they're driven by legibility.\n\nA common set of breakpoints:\n\n- **Large (200px+)** — Full lockup\n- **Medium (100–200px)** — Compact lockup or logotype removed\n- **Small (48–100px)** — Logomark only\n- **Tiny (16–48px)** — Simplified logomark\n\n![Logo breakpoints across sizes](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*GFkJB1v-E_rPlC8uQ6mXLw.png)\n\n## Step 4: Simplify progressively\n\nEach step down in size should remove detail, not add it. Common simplification strategies:\n\n- Remove the logotype (text)\n- Remove secondary colors\n- Thicken thin strokes that would disappear at small sizes\n- Reduce or remove internal detail in the symbol\n\n![Progressive logo simplification](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*Whr6G9dBcZKB66jQ-kw3-w.png)\n\nThe key: each simplified version must still feel like the same brand. If someone sees the favicon and doesn't connect it to the full logo, the simplification went wrong.\n\n## Step 5: Test in real contexts\n\nDesign is nothing without context. Test each version where it will actually live:\n\n- Browser tab (favicon)\n- Mobile home screen (app icon)\n- Social media avatar (circle crop)\n- Email signature\n- Website header (desktop and mobile)\n- Print: business card, letterhead, signage\n\n![Logo testing across real contexts](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*87JkeDIhDrFjp4V_iCSjdA.png)\n\n## Step 6: Document the system\n\nA responsive logo system is only useful if people know how to use it. Your brand guidelines should include:\n\n- Each version with its name\n- Minimum size for each version\n- Clear rules for when to switch between versions\n- Spacing and clearance requirements per version\n\n> A responsive logo isn't about making the logo smaller. It's about making the logo work at every size.\n\nThe brands that do this well — Google, Spotify, Chanel — have logo systems, not just logos. Every size has been considered, tested, and documented. That's the standard to aim for.","category":"Identité de marque","author":"Benjamin Oberemok","authorRole":"Creative Director","publishedAt":"Mar 1, 2021","readingTime":"6 min read","heroImage":"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1000/1*87JkeDIhDrFjp4V_iCSjdA.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":3,"searchTags":null},{"id":40,"slug":"vertical-logos-design-guide","title":"How to Create a Well-balanced Vertical Logo","excerpt":"The vertical version of a logo requires different proportions than the horizontal one. Here's how to scale the symbol correctly and avoid the most common mistakes.","body":"Some time ago, while working on branding for one of our clients, we had a little discussion about various options for building a logo. Since the horizontal version was first built, it was necessary to determine the proportions for the vertical. For the vertical version, it was necessary to change the proportions between the logotype and the symbol, but not everyone agreed on what exactly these proportions should be.\n\nThis may seem like a private matter, but it is not. Logo and branding designers are constantly confronted with this. Flipping through Dribbble, Behance and other resources where designers show their work, I often notice one common mistake in the logo. It is a mistake to make the logo mark in the vertical version of the logo the same size as in the horizontal one. Yes, I know that sometimes it works, but this is more of an exception than a rule.\n\nThe designer most often creates at first a horizontal, let's say the main version, and then simply places a symbol on top of the logotype to make the vertical version. Why do we need different logo versions and is it possible to get by with only one option? Let's try to figure this out.\n\n![Horizontal vs vertical logo comparison](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*2PgLReIKcT9GEfNFRUyXkg.png)\n\n## Logos yesterday and today\n\nTechnological advances have greatly changed the design industry over the past few decades. Before the era of digital technology, logos and other corporate identities were used only on physical objects, and changing them was neither easy nor cheap. But this situation is a thing of the past.\n\nToday we see an identity on websites and services, applications on a mobile phone and tablet, on social networks, all kinds of instant messengers and more. On one hand, it opened up enormous opportunities for designers, and on the other hand, it also imposed certain requirements. Thus, we inevitably come to such a concept as a \"responsive logo\". In this article, we will not dwell on responsive logos as much, but only touch on its individual points, so to speak, to understand where it came from.\n\n![Evolution of logo usage across platforms](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*6wEJN39tLACOnye2LmKGfA.png)\n\n## The main types of logo\n\nThe most common type of the logo consists of a symbol and text. More correctly sounds like logomark and logotype. The full version of the logo (logomark and logotype) is mainly used as the basic one, while the logomark, a brand identifier, is more often used as an avatar for social networks, instant messengers, etc.\n\n![Logomark and logotype components](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*smveQn1098jmPCYDqEEJlA.png)\n\nSometimes the logomark can work as the main version of the logo, where the text part is additional or, let's say, secondary. Examples include brands such as Starbucks, Apple, Shell, McDonald's or applications and services such as Snapchat, Evernote, Dropbox, Spark, Whatsapp, and others.\n\n![Famous brands using logomark as primary identity](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*Hs8KH1sJNA7v007v3J8B5g.jpeg)\n\nAlso, and vice versa, there are companies that do not use a symbol or sign. Companies such as Coca-cola, Jeep, Virgin, Netflix, Kellogg's use only the logotype.\n\n![Brands using logotype only](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*veFcx9NcxQRvCTOztzQh8g.png)\n\n## Horizontal and vertical versions of the logo\n\nIf we discard special cases, then the logo is usually developed in horizontal and vertical versions because of their different application purposes.\n\nThe horizontal version is mainly used on the website, letterhead, for creating business cards, advertising on billboards, advertising and sponsorship products.\n\nThe vertical version is used for square and vertical layout formats. For example, for printing typographic products, t-shirts, mugs, advertising banners, for instant messengers, social networks, signboards.\n\n## Instructions or principles?\n\nNow let's get back to the problem said at the beginning. Why does the vertical version of the logo with the same proportions as the horizontal one look uncertain or even bad? Why does the logomark in the vertical version most often have to be one and a half or two times bigger, and sometimes even three? You may ask where I got all this from and on the basis of what I draw such a conclusion.\n\nOf course, there does not exist and cannot be clear rules spelled out by someone of what sizes, shapes and proportions should designers strictly adhere to when developing a logo. Everything is very personal and hardly anyone will argue with this. However, there are principles of design and composition, guided by which we can separate the wheat from the tares.\n\nTherefore, based on the analysis of good and bad examples of logo design, I intend to derive certain principles and rules, guided by which it is more likely to get a good result.\n\n![Design principles illustrated](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*BUadvUuy_fp-jDKEHBgINg.png)\n\n## The bad news\n\nYou've probably often heard the phrase: \"I have good news and bad, which do you want to hear?\" Since you can't answer, I will start with the bad, to finish all the same on a good note.\n\nThe bad news is that even large brands with big design budgets make serious mistakes. One of these examples I recently found on Google. This is the Google Marketing Platform logo. I must say that the symbol itself is quite good, but the construction of the logo could be improved.\n\n![Google Marketing Platform logo analysis](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1000/1*ZwmCNHMyaeofsxFkOBh5ZQ.png)\n\nPerhaps most people may not notice anything terrible here, but this article is intended mainly for designers who should be able to distinguish good from bad.\n\nWhat distinguishes an amateur from a professional? An amateur understands that something is bad or lacking, but does not understand why. A professional, on the contrary, sees a problem and understands why it arose and how to solve it.\n\nSo, what is wrong with this logo. Here Google makes several severe errors at once.\n\nFirstly, it uses a too long title in one line, which is not perceived as a logo, but simply as a piece of text.\n\nSecondly, in the vertical version of the logo it leaves a symbol of the same size, which makes the situation with the perception of the logo even worse.\n\nI talk about this quite seriously, because this is the final version, these screenshots are taken from their official brand guidelines. When we at Unfold agency are working on a logo or branding, we make many unsuccessful versions in the process, but they are polished and verified for production (logo system).\n\nLet's try to fix the situation with the Google Marketing Platform logo.\n\n![Google Marketing Platform logo redesign](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*XchBPKfV1e_91WBI6hbIYQ.png)\n\n## Good news\n\nThe good news is that there are many designers who do an excellent job. Below I have made a selection from the works of one of the most popular logo designers on Dribbble: Jeroen van Eerden.\n\n![Well-balanced vertical logo examples by Jeroen van Eerden](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1000/1*qfR7JOcxDJbsHN_3irV57g.png)\n\nAs you may have noticed, on some vertical versions the symbol is more than doubled. With a significant increase in the symbol, the vertical version acquired a special harmony and expressiveness. Please note that the vertical version looks good when its proportions seem to tend to a square or fit into it, while the horizontal version tends to a rectangle.\n\nIn general, these are good examples of building a logo. However, there are cases in which it would look even better if you make some changes. Let's do it with Groupmarks as an example.\n\n![Groupmarks logo improvement](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*boWYBbs5wi00aZXyQuvkqQ.png)\n\nIn the original version, the distance between the symbol and the logotype is too small, as they say there is not enough air, it creates the feeling that the symbol on top seems to put pressure on the text. Therefore, we add air and slightly increase the symbol. As a result of small changes, the vertical version has become much more seamless and harmonious.\n\n![Improved spacing in vertical logo](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*Sx8IerRi_kdsJpNS5cIL9Q.png)\n\nThus, we can formulate the first principle, which states:\n\n> In the vertical version of the logo, you need to add more air between the symbol and the text than in the horizontal.\n\nThe scope of the vertical versions of the logo is usually square in vertical formats, as well as in large spaces. Therefore, in order to make the logo look expressive and \"solemn,\" one does not need to be afraid to enlarge the symbol.\n\nTherefore, the following principle sounds like this:\n\n> The horizontal version of the logo needs to be confined to a rectangle, while the vertical version to a square.\n\n![Logo proportions — rectangle vs square](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*148XemZajik_QiQ7Cjgq1w.png)\n\n## How to build a vertical version of the logo\n\nThe vertical version looks confident when it fits into a square, we have already figured that out. The most important question here is how much you need to scale the symbol to get a good balanced result.\n\nIn this matter, at least the following three parameters must be considered:\n\n1. Logo length\n2. Logo thickness\n3. Color activity of the symbol\n\n## 1. Logo length\n\nThe length of the logo is sometimes quite inconvenient for the development of the logo. The longer the logo, the more difficult it is to build a balanced solution. With short logos, the symbol can be made large enough, which will well affect the proportions of the vertical version of the logo (fits into the square). If the logo is long, then the vertical version is much more difficult to build so that it fits into the square because in this case the text will be very small, and not legible.\n\nBelow are the successful solutions with different logo lengths.\n\n![Logo with a long logotype — symbol increases by ~130%](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*4AlTOgQgwhzppKVnMMxzDA.png)\n\n![Logo with a medium length logotype — symbol increases by ~140%](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*fQH7bbbef-XlQ1b1o9bJoA.png)\n\n![Logo with medium and short logotype — symbol increases by ~180-200%](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*4Pbu4VcaJsjJeDl_orbR7Q.png)\n\n![Logo with a short logotype — symbol increases by ~200%](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*JIZGB23bCrD4pfkf0ISneQ.png)\n\nAs you already understood, the shorter the logo, the larger the symbol. This, of course, is not an axiom, but a good rule adhering to which the logo will look confident.\n\n## 2. Logo thickness\n\nThe next parameter that helps us correctly scale the symbol in the vertical version of the logo is the thickness of the logotype.\n\nIn the examples below, you can see that the symbol in the vertical version must be increased more when the logotype is thin. Just like with logo length, the thinner the text, the more the symbol needs to grow to maintain visual balance.\n\n![Thin logotype requiring larger symbol increase](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*RkOL9tXKqLWiXV38Dh_BsA.png)\n\nConversely, if the logotype is set in a bold or heavy weight, the symbol can remain closer to its original size because the text already carries enough visual mass.\n\n## 3. Color activity of the symbol\n\nThe last parameter to consider is the color activity (visual density) of the symbol. A solid, filled symbol has more visual weight than an outlined one. A multicolored symbol draws more attention than a monochrome one.\n\n![Color activity comparison in logo symbols](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*YiVV_5PXcLaFE1hFMaQC2g.png)\n\nIf your symbol is visually active — colorful, filled, detailed — it doesn't need to be scaled up as much in the vertical version. Its inherent visual weight already commands attention.\n\nIf your symbol is minimal — thin lines, monochrome, geometric — it needs more scaling to hold its own above the logotype.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nBuilding a vertical version of a logo is not as simple as placing the symbol on top of the text. It requires careful consideration of proportions, spacing, and visual weight.\n\nThe three key principles we've established:\n\n> Add more air between the symbol and text in the vertical version than in the horizontal.\n\n> The horizontal version should fit into a rectangle, while the vertical version should tend toward a square.\n\n> Scale the symbol based on logo length, logotype thickness, and the color activity of the symbol.\n\nBy following these guidelines, you'll create vertical logo versions that feel balanced, intentional, and professional — not like an afterthought.","category":"Identité de marque","author":"Benjamin Oberemok","authorRole":"Creative Director","publishedAt":"May 11, 2020","readingTime":"10 min read","heroImage":"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1000/1*mHrs20wHljeaoCLnKq363g.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":4,"searchTags":null},{"id":41,"slug":"figma-plugins-for-designers","title":"Top 10 Figma Plugins (For Now)","excerpt":"The best Figma plugins we use at Unfold right now. From icon libraries to content generation, these are the tools that save us hours every week.","body":"A while ago, Figma launched a brand new feature — plugins! The community has been buzzing with new tools ever since, and we couldn't be more excited. Over the past few months, we've put dozens of plugins to the test, and now we'd like to share our top 10 picks.\n\nSince new plugins keep popping up, our list might change — hence the \"for now\" in the title. But at this moment, these are the ones that have genuinely improved our workflow.\n\n![Figma plugins overview](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1400/1*37kywbT5OKIMAbJbpNV-hQ.png)\n\n## 1. Iconify\n\nLet's start with something almost every designer needs — icons. Iconify is hands down one of the most useful Figma plugins available. It gives you access to a massive collection of icon sets, all searchable and insertable directly into your Figma file. No more switching between browser tabs to download SVGs.\n\n![Iconify plugin interface](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*QfPdOsBpqNUJnR5eqkRp6g.png)\n\nThe library includes Material Design, Font Awesome, Feather, and many more. You can search by keyword, browse by set, and adjust the size before inserting. It's the icon tool we reach for first.\n\n## 2. Unsplash\n\nNeed placeholder images? Unsplash for Figma makes it effortless. Browse the entire Unsplash library without leaving Figma, and insert high-resolution photos directly into your frames.\n\n![Unsplash plugin in action](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*cAk5kKIXS-tTZH3q3xUsmg.png)\n\nIt's faster than searching on the website and dragging images in. Select a layer, run the plugin, search for what you need, and click to fill. Done.\n\n## 3. Content Reel\n\nFilling designs with realistic content is crucial for client presentations. Content Reel lets you populate your designs with real text strings, avatars, and other content data.\n\n![Content Reel plugin demo](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*G7qBl-XjnFODXKd2Nnw2Pg.png)\n\nNames, emails, addresses, avatars — all populated in a click. You can even create your own custom data sets for project-specific content. We use it in almost every project at the wireframing stage.\n\n## 4. Stark\n\nAccessibility shouldn't be an afterthought. Stark helps you check color contrast ratios right inside Figma, ensuring your designs meet WCAG standards.\n\n![Stark accessibility checker](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*a9IVaEafKpIjOGrJCxVhPg.png)\n\nIt also simulates color blindness, so you can see how your design appears to users with different types of color vision. This one's a must-have for any designer who takes accessibility seriously.\n\n## 5. Rename It\n\nOrganizing layers is tedious but necessary. Rename It lets you batch rename layers using variables like width, height, and sequential numbering.\n\n![Rename It batch layer renaming](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*JRp-CwbZBpO_YkxfqzGBEA.png)\n\nSelect a bunch of layers, define your naming pattern, and apply. It turns a 20-minute cleanup task into a 20-second one. When you're handing off files to developers, clean layer names are not optional — they're professional.\n\n## 6. Blobs\n\nSometimes you need organic, random shapes for backgrounds or decorative elements. Blobs generates customizable blob shapes that you can adjust for complexity and contrast.\n\n![Blobs plugin generating organic shapes](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*I5TaLQzJQB5M0vSxNwjR8g.png)\n\nIt's surprisingly useful for landing pages, illustrations, and abstract compositions. Adjust the number of points, randomness, and size to get exactly the shape you need.\n\n## 7. Image Palette\n\nNeed to extract a color palette from a photo? Image Palette analyzes any image in your file and generates a set of colors from it.\n\n![Image Palette color extraction](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*h7a4ycTW1CuYZ8XXPC12Ag.png)\n\nWe use this when working with photography-heavy projects where the color scheme needs to harmonize with the imagery. It pulls the dominant and accent colors, which you can then apply to your design tokens.\n\n## 8. Wireframe\n\nFor rapid prototyping, the Wireframe plugin provides a library of pre-built wireframe components. Drag in navigation bars, content blocks, footers, and other standard patterns.\n\n![Wireframe plugin component library](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*WRLLf7NfPkJHuNaxJp4a6w.png)\n\nIt's not a replacement for custom wireframing, but when you need to sketch out a concept quickly, it saves considerable time.\n\n## 9. LottieFiles\n\nIf your projects involve animation (and they should), LottieFiles lets you preview, test, and insert Lottie animations directly in Figma.\n\n![LottieFiles animation preview](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*GDPxPzBvVA7J2M-0-VnSag.png)\n\nYou can browse their library of free animations, preview them in context, and export the JSON files for your developers. It bridges the gap between static design and motion design.\n\n## 10. Map Maker\n\nLast but not least — maps. If your design includes location-based features, Map Maker generates real map images using Mapbox or Google Maps.\n\n![Map Maker generating real maps](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*4IqL2WHDqSS3VTIcxCnhDg.png)\n\nEnter an address, choose your style (standard, satellite, dark mode), set the zoom level, and insert. No more taking screenshots of Google Maps and cropping them into your designs.\n\n## Wrapping up\n\nPlugins are still a relatively new addition to Figma, and the ecosystem is growing fast. Some of these might get replaced by better alternatives in the coming months — that's the nature of tools. But right now, these ten cover the majority of our daily needs at Unfold.\n\n> The best plugin is the one that disappears into your workflow. You don't think about it — you just use it.\n\nIf you have a favorite Figma plugin that didn't make our list, we'd love to hear about it. The design community is at its best when we share what works.","category":"Design produit","author":"David Kovalev","authorRole":"Design Lead","publishedAt":"Aug 16, 2019","readingTime":"6 min read","heroImage":"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1400/1*37kywbT5OKIMAbJbpNV-hQ.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":5,"searchTags":null},{"id":25,"slug":"ai-as-creative-partner","title":"AI as Creative Partner: Machine Intelligence Meets Human Craft","excerpt":"AI isn't replacing designers — it's amplifying them. From mood board generation to rapid concepting, here's how creative agencies are integrating AI tools without sacrificing the craft clients expect.","body":"For years now, designers have been split into two camps on AI: the \"this changes everything!\" crowd and the \"robots are stealing our jobs\" crowd. Both are wrong, and honestly? Both are kind of annoying.\n\nThere's a middle ground, and it's where the magic happens.\n\nThe best creative teams we know — including ours — use AI every single day. Not as a replacement for thinking, but as a way to skip the tedious stuff and get to the interesting work faster. That distinction is everything.\n\n## Where AI Actually Helps (Like, For Real)\n\nLet's get specific. AI isn't some vague \"future of design\" — it's useful in very concrete ways, right now, today:\n\n**Mood boards and style exploration.** This one's a game-changer. What used to eat up half a day — finding reference images, cropping them, arranging them into something presentable — now takes minutes. You describe a direction, the AI generates visual territories, and suddenly you've got three rich mood boards to show the team before lunch. Does it replace your curatorial eye? No. But it gives you way more raw material to curate from, and that's genuinely powerful.\n\n**Early-stage concepting.** You know that phase where you're staring at a blank Figma file, trying to figure out where to even start? AI is perfect for that moment. Throw ideas at it — type pairings, color combos, layout structures, illustration styles — and let it give you a bunch of rough directions. Most of them will be mediocre. A few will spark something real. The key thing to remember: AI generates options. You make choices. Knowing *why* one direction feels right and another falls flat? That's the irreducibly human part.\n\n**First drafts of copy.** Whether it's website headlines, brand positioning statements, or just placeholder text that doesn't make you cringe, AI gets you from \"blank page\" to \"working draft\" absurdly fast. The output always needs a human pass — tone, nuance, knowing your audience — but the acceleration from zero to something is real.\n\n**Quick prototyping.** Need to test an idea before committing? AI tools can spin up functional prototypes in hours, not days. More ideas tested, more variations explored, better decisions made. This is especially clutch for product design and web projects where you need stakeholder buy-in before going deep.\n\n## The Stuff AI Can't Touch\n\nHere's where it gets interesting — and where your job is safe.\n\nAI cannot understand your business. It can't feel the tension between what a founder says they want and what their company actually needs. It can't make the judgment call that a certain shade of blue says \"trustworthy\" in fintech but \"cold and clinical\" in healthcare.\n\nIt doesn't understand politics, ego, organizational dynamics, or why the CEO's favorite color matters even when it shouldn't. It can't read a room during a client presentation and pivot the narrative on the fly.\n\nThese are the moments where design becomes strategy, and strategy becomes the thing clients actually pay for. No model handles this. Honestly? No model should.\n\n## A Real Example: How This Plays Out\n\nWe recently worked on a rebrand for a health-tech company. The AI tools helped us generate 40+ logo concepts in an afternoon — different typographic approaches, symbol explorations, color palette variations. That would've taken a designer three days to sketch out manually.\n\nBut here's the thing: about 35 of those concepts were trash. Not because the AI is bad, but because it doesn't know that the healthcare space is oversaturated with certain visual tropes (hello, generic leaf/human/cross combos). A junior designer with six months of experience would know to avoid half of those directions. The AI had no idea.\n\nSo the real value wasn't the 40 options. It was that we got to the 5 interesting ones by 2pm instead of Thursday. The strategic work — choosing the right direction, understanding the competitive landscape, presenting the rationale — that was all us. But we had three extra days to do it well.\n\n## The Vibe Check\n\nHere's our internal rule of thumb: if a task requires taste, empathy, strategy, or persuasion, a human does it. If it requires speed, volume, pattern-matching, or grunt work, AI does it. The overlap between those two categories is surprisingly small.\n\nThe teams that'll do best in the next few years aren't the ones using the most AI or the least. They're the ones who know exactly where the line is between what should be automated and what should be agonized over.\n\nThat line? It's the whole game.\n\n## What We Tell Our Clients\n\nWhen clients ask us about AI, we're honest. We tell them we use it. We tell them where. And we tell them that every final deliverable is authored, reviewed, and refined by a human designer who understands their brand, their market, and their goals.\n\nWe're not outsourcing our thinking. We're outsourcing our admin. And that distinction — between thinking and admin — is what separates the teams that'll thrive from the ones that'll drown in mediocre AI output and call it \"efficiency.\"\n\nThe craft is safe. It's the busywork that's in trouble. And honestly? Good riddance.","category":"AI & Design","author":"Julian Vane","authorRole":"Principal Strategy","publishedAt":"Jan 28, 2026","readingTime":"6 min read","heroImage":"/images/case-studies/brainfm-1.png","featured":true,"displayOrder":6,"searchTags":null},{"id":31,"slug":"death-of-rounded-corner","title":"The Death of the Rounded Corner","excerpt":"Why premium digital products are returning to the sharp, brutal precision of print layout. An editorial on the future of interface design and brand identity.","body":"For the better part of a decade, the digital world got soft. Like, *really* soft. iOS squircles. Bubbly web app containers. Rounded buttons with rounded shadows inside rounded cards. Everything looked like it was designed to be hugged.\n\nWe were told that sharp edges were \"aggressive.\" That rounded corners were more \"human.\" That our brains just *preferred* the gentle arc. And sure, there's some truth to the psychology. But somewhere along the way, \"user-friendly\" became \"visual baby food,\" and nobody stopped to ask if we'd gone too far.\n\nSpoiler: we had.\n\n> \"Precision is the new empathy. In a world of digital noise, the sharp edge is a return to architectural intent.\"\n\n## The Swiss Had It Right\n\nWhen you look at the history of high-end design — Swiss typography, Bauhaus industrial design, editorial print — you see a reverence for the right angle. There's a brutal honesty in a 90-degree corner. It demands alignment. It forces the designer to commit. It says: \"I know exactly where this element starts and where it ends.\"\n\nRounded corners, by contrast, are forgiving. They hide imprecision. They let you get away with sloppy spacing because the eye glosses over soft edges. And that's exactly why they became the default — not because they're better, but because they're *easier*.\n\n## Why This Matters Now\n\nHere's what's happening: the industries that care most about trust — fintech, blockchain, enterprise SaaS, healthcare — are leading the return to sharp edges. And it makes sense. If you're building a product that handles people's money, health data, or business operations, you don't want it to feel like a toy. You want it to feel like an instrument. Instruments have edges.\n\nLook at Linear. Look at Notion. Look at the editorial precision of platforms like Bloomberg Terminal or the Stripe dashboard. These are products that respect their users enough to present information cleanly, without softening it. The sharp corner says: \"this is exactly where this thing is. No ambiguity.\"\n\n## The Grid Reclamation\n\nThere's a deeper design principle at play here. When everything is rounded, the grid becomes *suggestive*. Elements float in approximate positions. Alignment is implied rather than enforced. The grid is more of a gentle recommendation than a structural foundation.\n\nWhen everything is sharp? The grid is law. And that has implications across every design discipline — brand, web, product, illustration. The sharpness forces precision, and precision is what separates considered design from decoration.\n\n## An Anecdote\n\nWe worked on a fintech dashboard last year where the client's existing product used 16px border-radius on everything. Every card, every button, every input field — all softened, all friendly. The product looked fine. It also looked exactly like every other SaaS tool on the market.\n\nWhen we presented a direction with zero border-radius — sharp corners everywhere, tight grid, precise spacing — the client's first reaction was \"this feels cold.\" But after living with it for a week, they came back and said something interesting: \"it feels more *serious*. Like software that actually knows what it's doing.\"\n\nThat reaction tells you everything. The rounded corner signals approachability. The sharp corner signals competence. And for products where competence is the brand promise, the choice is obvious.\n\n## But Wait — What About Consumer Apps?\n\nFair point. The rounded corner isn't dead everywhere. It still has its place in consumer apps designed for casual engagement — social media, messaging, lifestyle apps. Places where the brand promise is \"this is easy and fun.\" A dating app with sharp corners would feel weird. A meditation app with harsh edges would be off-brand.\n\nThe distinction isn't about one being better than the other. It's about intentionality. The problem with rounded corners isn't that they exist — it's that they became a default. A non-choice. The designer who uses sharp corners today is making a *statement*. The designer who uses rounded corners is usually just using whatever Tailwind or Material Design gave them out of the box.\n\n## The Pendulum\n\nDesign trends are cyclical, and we know the sharp corner won't reign forever. But this particular swing feels different because it's driven by substance, not just style. The return to precision, to the grid, to architectural intent — it's a correction against a decade of visual softness that prioritized \"friendly\" over \"clear.\"\n\nIn the world of premium digital products — brand identity systems, enterprise dashboards, editorial web experiences — the future has sharp edges. And they look damn good.","category":"Design Philosophy","author":"Julian Vane","authorRole":"Principal Strategy","publishedAt":"Oct 12, 2025","readingTime":"4 min read","heroImage":"/images/case-studies/xotc-1.png","featured":true,"displayOrder":7,"searchTags":null},{"id":32,"slug":"web3-brand-identity","title":"Building Brand Identity in Web3: Lessons from the Frontier","excerpt":"Web3 brands face a unique challenge: communicating trust in a trustless ecosystem. What the best identities in decentralized spaces get right — and what most get wrong.","body":"Remember when every Web3 project looked like it was designed inside a Blade Runner screenshot? Neon gradients, angular sci-fi type, abstract particle effects, maybe a hexagon or twelve. It was exciting for about five minutes and then it became wallpaper. You couldn't tell one DeFi protocol from another if your life depended on it.\n\nThe good news? That era is mostly over. The Web3 ecosystem has grown up, and with it, the branding is finally catching up.\n\n## The Trust Paradox (It's a Real Thing)\n\nHere's the fundamental weirdness of Web3 branding: the entire technology is built on *trustlessness* — cryptographic verification replacing the need to trust institutions. But the brands that actually win in this space? They're the ones that *feel* trustworthy.\n\nThe code might be trustless, but the humans choosing to interact with it aren't. They're scared, excited, confused, hopeful — and they're looking for signals that say \"you can rely on this.\" That's what a brand does. It bridges the gap between \"I verified the smart contract\" and \"I feel good about using this product.\"\n\nThis creates a genuinely fascinating design challenge. How do you build a brand that communicates reliability within an ecosystem that was designed to eliminate the need for reliability? You can't fake it with gradients. You need real strategy — naming, positioning, visual systems that go way deeper than surface aesthetics.\n\n## Visual Maturity > Visual Novelty\n\nThe early Web3 aesthetic was all novelty: \"look how futuristic and different we are!\" The problem is that when everyone is trying to look futuristic and different in the exact same way, nobody looks futuristic or different. Every project was designed in the same Figma file using the same gradient overlays.\n\nThe strongest Web3 brands today have moved past this. They use:\n- Clean geometry and confident color instead of gratuitous glow\n- Illustration systems that feel human, not algorithmic\n- Typography that prioritizes readability over \"cool\"\n- Restraint — the radical act of not putting a gradient on everything\n\nWe worked with a Solana-based project last year that came to us with a full brand kit dripping in neon purple and electric blue. Their feedback from users? \"It looks cool but I don't trust it.\" We stripped 80% of the visual complexity, introduced a structured type system, and used color sparingly and with purpose. Trust scores in user testing went up 40%. No joke.\n\nThe key is a rigorous style discovery process. You can use AI-powered mood board generation to quickly explore directions, but the decisions about what feels distinctive vs. derivative — that's human judgment, every time.\n\n## Community Is the Brand\n\nThis is where Web3 branding gets genuinely different from traditional brand work. In traditional branding, the company controls the narrative. In Web3, the community *is* the narrative.\n\nThat fundamentally changes how you build identity systems. The best Web3 brands are designed for participation — flexible enough that community members can adopt, remix, and extend them without breaking the core brand. Think badges, PFP templates, social kits, meme-friendly assets. The brand gets *stronger* as more people use it, not weaker.\n\nThis requires brand guidelines that are permissive rather than restrictive. Instead of \"never use the logo below 24px\" and \"always maintain 2x clear space,\" you need guidelines like \"here are the elements you can remix freely\" and \"here are the few things that should stay consistent.\"\n\nIt's a massive shift from traditional corporate identity work, and it's honestly more fun to design for.\n\n## What We've Learned From Doing This\n\nAfter working on brands for multiple chains, DeFi protocols, and NFT platforms, here's what we've figured out:\n\n**Names matter more than you think.** A forgettable name can't be saved by good design. Invest in naming strategy before you touch a pixel.\n\n**Your brand needs to work at 32x32 pixels.** Discord avatar, browser favicon, mobile app icon — if your brand doesn't read at tiny sizes, it doesn't work. Period.\n\n**Don't underestimate print.** Yes, even in Web3. Conference badges, sticker packs, merch — physical brand touchpoints build loyalty faster than any Discord channel.\n\n**Consistency compounds.** The projects that build recognition fastest are the ones that show up the same way everywhere. Same voice, same visual system, same level of craft. It's boring advice, but it's the most important advice.\n\n## The Opportunity\n\nWeb3 brand design is still one of the most exciting areas in the industry. The stakes are genuinely high — bad branding can tank a token launch, good branding can turn a protocol into a movement. The audiences are sophisticated, the feedback loops are fast, and the design decisions have real financial consequences.\n\nFor creative teams willing to invest in the strategic depth this space demands, the opportunity is massive. Just leave the neon gradients at the door.","category":"Web3 & Crypto","author":"Maya Chen","authorRole":"Creative Director","publishedAt":"Sep 22, 2025","readingTime":"5 min read","heroImage":"/images/case-studies/safepal-hero.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":8,"searchTags":null},{"id":26,"slug":"ai-brand-strategy","title":"How AI Is Reshaping Brand Strategy — and Why Taste Still Wins","excerpt":"AI can generate a thousand logos in an hour. But can it tell you which one will still resonate in ten years? Where AI accelerates brand strategy and where human judgment remains the competitive edge.","body":"The most dangerous thing happening in branding right now isn't AI itself. It's the idea that AI can replace the *thinking* behind a brand.\n\nAI is incredibly useful for brand work — naming explorations, competitive audits, mood boards, tagline generation, visual style discovery. It's reshaping the speed at which teams operate. But the places where AI excels and the places where brands are actually *built* are not the same places.\n\n## Speed vs. Taste\n\nAI has compressed what we call the \"speed layer.\" Competitive analysis that used to take days? Done in an afternoon. Naming brainstorms? You can generate 200 options before your coffee gets cold. Visual mood boards? Minutes, not hours.\n\nBut here's the thing about speed: it gets you to the starting line faster. It doesn't tell you which direction to run.\n\nConsider a typical brand engagement: mapping the visual language of 200+ competitors. AI knocks that out quickly. But the output isn't a strategy — it's raw material. Strategy comes from people with taste, experience, and the confidence to say \"I know this looks different, but it's right.\"\n\n## What Taste Actually Means\n\nTaste gets thrown around a lot, so let's be specific. Taste is the ability to look at something that is technically correct and know that it's emotionally wrong. It's the instinct that says \"this color palette is gorgeous, but it's wrong for this audience.\" It's what separates a brand people *like* from a brand people *love*.\n\nThe best brand decisions come from a moment where someone says \"the data supports this direction, but my gut says otherwise.\" That gut isn't mysticism — it's pattern recognition built over years of shipping real work to real audiences.\n\nNo model has that. No model is close.\n\n## How Smart Teams Use Both\n\nThe best teams aren't choosing between AI and human judgment. They're building workflows where AI handles the breadth — exploring tons of directions quickly — and humans handle the depth — choosing the one that has a soul.\n\nAI can show you every possible direction. Only a human can tell you which one actually *means* something.\n\nBe skeptical of any team that says AI is doing their thinking. Be equally skeptical of any team that says they don't use it at all. The truth, as always, is in the craft of integration.","category":"AI & Design","author":"Julian Vane","authorRole":"Principal Strategy","publishedAt":"Jan 14, 2026","readingTime":"5 min read","heroImage":"/images/case-studies/wonder-1.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":9,"searchTags":null},{"id":28,"slug":"generative-ai-design-ethics","title":"The Ethics of Generative Design: Where Should Agencies Draw the Line?","excerpt":"Generative AI can produce stunning visuals in seconds. But when does efficiency cross into ethical gray area? A framework for responsible AI use in creative work.","body":"Every week there's a new AI image generator. Every week the output gets better — more photorealistic, more stylistically flexible, more \"good enough\" to ship. And every week, creative teams have to answer the same uncomfortable question: where's the line?\n\nWe've thought about this a lot. Here's where we've landed.\n\n## Be Honest With Clients\n\nIf AI contributed to a deliverable — mood boards, rough concepts, copy drafts — the client should know. Not because of legal stuff (though that matters too), but because of trust. Clients hire you for your judgment and your process. Being transparent about your tools is part of respecting that relationship.\n\n## AI Assists, It Doesn't Author\n\nThere's a real difference between using AI to explore a color palette and using AI to design a logo. The first is a tool helping a creative process. The second is outsourcing authorship. We use AI for the former, never the latter.\n\nEvery final deliverable — brand identity, custom illustrations, product interfaces — gets authored by a human designer. AI might have helped them explore faster, but the creative decisions that define the work? Those are human.\n\n## Care About Training Data\n\nNot all AI tools are created equal. The ones that transparently license their training data or use opt-in datasets deserve your preference. The ones with opaque or contested data sources? Use them cautiously, if at all. This matters because the creative community we're part of matters.\n\n## Client Work Stays Private\n\nThis should be obvious, but: never feed client projects, briefs, brand assets, or strategy docs into public AI models. Ever. Client work stays in secure, private environments. Full stop.\n\n## The Bigger Picture\n\nIn a world where anyone can generate a beautiful image in seconds, the competitive advantage isn't generation. It's judgment, integrity, and craft. Those things aren't automatable. And that's exactly the point.","category":"AI & Design","author":"Julian Vane","authorRole":"Principal Strategy","publishedAt":"Dec 3, 2025","readingTime":"6 min read","heroImage":"/images/case-studies/zed-1.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":10,"searchTags":null},{"id":34,"slug":"design-systems-scale","title":"Design Systems That Actually Scale: Building for Real Organizations","excerpt":"Most design systems die within a year. Here's why — and the principles that separate systems built to last from expensive shelf-ware.","body":"Let's be honest: design systems are the New Year's resolutions of product design. Everyone starts one with great intentions, and most are abandoned by March.\n\nThe ones that survive share a few traits. The ones that die share a few different traits. Let's talk about both.\n\n## Why They Fail\n\n**The 200-page doc nobody reads.** If your design system requires a manual, it's too complex. The best systems are self-documenting — components communicate their purpose through clear naming, sensible defaults, and constrained props. If a designer needs to read a Notion page to figure out which button to use, you've already lost.\n\n**Nobody actually uses it.** A design system nobody adopts is just a component library on life support. Adoption fails when the system doesn't solve real problems, when it's too rigid for edge cases, or when the team wasn't involved in building it. That last one is the big one — if you build a system *for* a team instead of *with* a team, don't be surprised when they ignore it.\n\n**Perfectionism.** Design systems that launch at \"v1.0\" with full coverage are almost always built in isolation. They're beautiful, thorough, and completely disconnected from how the product team actually works. They look great in the case study and collect dust in practice.\n\n## What Actually Works\n\n**Start from the mess.** Don't build from first principles. Build from the actual product. Find the places where inconsistency is causing real problems — where three different button styles exist, where spacing is random, where color usage makes no sense. Start there. Fix the pain points first.\n\n**Ship small, ship often.** The best systems grow organically. Ship foundational components — typography, color, spacing, buttons, inputs — and let the system evolve as the product evolves. Each new component should be born from a real need, not a theoretical checklist.\n\n**Let AI help with the exploration.** AI tools are genuinely useful for design system work. Style generation can quickly visualize how component variations behave across themes. Content generation helps populate realistic test cases. Quick prototyping lets you test components in realistic scenarios before finalizing specs. These tools speed up the iteration loop without sacrificing the deliberate decision-making that good systems require.\n\n## The Real Payoff\n\nA good design system doesn't just create consistency. It creates speed. When designers and developers share a common vocabulary and a trusted component library, they move faster. The product gets better because the team focuses on new problems instead of re-solving old ones.\n\nThe real value of a design system isn't the pixels. It's the time.","category":"Design","author":"Maya Chen","authorRole":"Creative Director","publishedAt":"Aug 20, 2025","readingTime":"6 min read","heroImage":"/images/case-studies/lmnft-1.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":11,"searchTags":null},{"id":30,"slug":"ai-typography-future","title":"Can AI Design a Typeface? The Future of Computational Typography","excerpt":"AI-generated typefaces are getting better. But typography is more than letterforms — it's culture, history, and intention compressed into curves. Where does AI fit?","body":"Typography might be the most culturally loaded discipline in all of design. Every curve and counter in a typeface carries centuries of aesthetic evolution, regional identity, and functional intent. So when AI enters the typography conversation, the stakes feel uniquely high.\n\n## What AI Can Actually Do Right Now\n\nCurrent AI tools can generate letterforms that are technically solid. Given enough training data, a model can produce glyphs with consistent stroke weight, proper optical spacing, and reasonable x-height relationships. Some outputs are genuinely usable for display purposes.\n\nBut \"usable\" and \"meaningful\" are very different standards. The best typefaces in history — Garamond, Helvetica, Futura, Apercu — weren't just well-drawn. They were *arguments*. They embodied philosophical positions about clarity, modernity, warmth, or authority. They were made by people with opinions about the world.\n\nAI doesn't have opinions. It has training data.\n\n## Where AI Is Genuinely Helpful\n\nThere are some practical areas where AI is already making typography work faster:\n\n**Weight interpolation.** Generating smooth transitions between light, regular, bold, and heavy weights — traditionally tedious manual work — can be accelerated significantly with ML models.\n\n**Glyph expansion.** Starting from a small set of designed characters and extrapolating the rest of the alphabet is now feasible. Not perfect, but a real time-saver for initial exploration.\n\n**Cross-language testing.** Checking how a typeface performs across Latin, Cyrillic, Arabic, and CJK scripts is dramatically faster with AI-assisted tooling.\n\nFor agencies, this means testing typographic solutions more efficiently. You can quickly visualize how different type choices behave across touchpoints — logo lockups, web headers, packaging, app interfaces — and give clients a faster path to confident decisions.\n\n## Where AI Hits a Wall\n\nA typeface is a voice. Choosing one is one of the most consequential decisions in brand design. It communicates before a single word is read. AI can generate a technically sound typeface. It can't imbue it with cultural meaning, strategic intent, or emotional resonance.\n\nThe future of computational typography isn't AI replacing type designers. It's AI handling the mechanical complexity so designers can focus on the cultural and aesthetic decisions that make typography *matter*.","category":"Design","author":"Julian Vane","authorRole":"Principal Strategy","publishedAt":"Nov 2, 2025","readingTime":"5 min read","heroImage":"/images/case-studies/fb-8.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":12,"searchTags":null},{"id":27,"slug":"ai-client-presentations","title":"From Brief to Board: AI and the Art of the Client Presentation","excerpt":"AI is transforming how creative agencies prepare and deliver client presentations. Not by making them — but by freeing teams to focus on the narrative that makes clients believe in the work.","body":"Here's an agency secret: senior creatives spend a wild amount of time on presentation logistics. Formatting decks. Writing transition slides. Summarizing research. Organizing feedback. None of it is creative work. All of it is necessary.\n\nAI is making that invisible.\n\n## Where It Actually Helps\n\n**Scaffolding the narrative.** Given a set of deliverables, AI can help outline the presentation flow — what to show first, where to pause, how to frame the rationale. You'll always rewrite it, but starting from a scaffold beats staring at a blank slide.\n\n**Mood board assembly.** When presenting brand directions, context is everything. AI assembles visual references fast — so clients can see their brand positioned within a visual landscape instead of floating in a vacuum.\n\n**Interactive previews.** For product and web presentations, AI-powered prototyping lets you show something *working* instead of something static. \"Experience this\" beats \"imagine this\" every time.\n\n## The Actual Win\n\nThe quality of presentations hasn't changed. The depth has. Less time on logistics means more time on the story. And the best presentation isn't the prettiest deck — it's the one where the client feels understood.","category":"AI & Design","author":"Maya Chen","authorRole":"Creative Director","publishedAt":"Dec 19, 2025","readingTime":"5 min read","heroImage":"/images/case-studies/tavus-1.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":13,"searchTags":null},{"id":29,"slug":"ai-ux-research","title":"AI-Powered UX Research: Faster Insights Without Cutting Corners","excerpt":"How AI tools are transforming user research, behavioral analysis, and the translation of findings into product design decisions that actually ship.","body":"User research is the conscience of good product design. It keeps you honest — forcing a confrontation between what you *assume* users want and what they actually need. But traditional research is slow and expensive, and insights often show up too late to shape the design.\n\nAI is speeding that up considerably.\n\n## Where AI Shines\n\n**Synthesis.** A single usability study generates hours of recordings and thousands of words of transcripts. AI tools can surface patterns, cluster themes, and flag outliers in a fraction of the time. This doesn't eliminate the researcher — it eliminates the bottleneck between collecting data and finding insights.\n\n**Rapid prototyping from findings.** Research insights can now be translated into testable prototypes within days instead of weeks. Tighter feedback loops between research and design = better products.\n\n## Where AI Falls Short\n\nAI can tell you users are dropping off at step three. It can't tell you *why*. It can't feel the frustration of a confusing interface or understand the emotional context of a financial transaction. The empathy part — the qualitative interpretation — is still entirely human.\n\nAI handles the quantitative backbone. Humans provide the qualitative depth. Together, they make research that's both rigorous and resonant.","category":"AI & Design","author":"Sana Adler","authorRole":"UX Research Lead","publishedAt":"Nov 18, 2025","readingTime":"5 min read","heroImage":"/images/case-studies/keycare-1.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":14,"searchTags":null},{"id":33,"slug":"motion-design-attention-economy","title":"Motion Design in the Attention Economy: When Animation Earns Its Place","excerpt":"Animation can delight or distract. In an era of shrinking attention spans, the best product and web design uses motion with purpose — not decoration.","body":"There's a disease in modern interface design: animation for animation's sake. Bouncing buttons. Spinning loaders that outlast the actual load. Parallax effects that look cool but make the page harder to use. Motion that exists because someone could, not because someone should.\n\nThe test for any animation is dead simple: does it *earn its place*?\n\n## The Earned Animation Checklist\n\nEvery animation should satisfy at least one of these:\n\n**Orientation.** Does it help the user understand where they are? Page transitions, nav state changes, scroll reveals — these answer \"what just happened?\"\n\n**Feedback.** Does it confirm an action? Button presses, form submissions, state changes — the user needs to know the system heard them.\n\n**Hierarchy.** Does it guide attention? Staggered reveals and focal animations say \"look here first.\"\n\n**Delight.** Does it create genuine pleasure *without* slowing things down? This is the rarest and most dangerous category. Delight that adds friction isn't delightful — it's indulgent.\n\nIf an animation doesn't hit at least one of these? Kill it.\n\n## Less, But Better\n\nThe best motion design is the motion you don't notice. The subtle ease-out that makes a transition feel natural. The 200ms fade that prevents a jarring swap. The micro-interaction that confirms your tap without demanding attention.\n\nIn the attention economy, restraint is a superpower.","category":"Design","author":"Sana Adler","authorRole":"UX Research Lead","publishedAt":"Sep 5, 2025","readingTime":"5 min read","heroImage":"/images/case-studies/rumble-hero.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":15,"searchTags":null},{"id":35,"slug":"spatial-computing-design","title":"Designing for Spatial Computing: What It Means for Brand Experience","excerpt":"Spatial computing isn't the future anymore — it's shipping. How forward-thinking brands should prepare their identity systems, illustrations, and product experiences for three dimensions.","body":"Apple Vision Pro shipped. Meta Quest keeps iterating. Spatial computing went from \"future tech\" to \"consumer product,\" and with it comes a design challenge most creative teams aren't ready for: extending a brand into three dimensions.\n\n## The Rectangle Is Dead (Sort Of)\n\nFor decades, design lived inside rectangles. Different sizes, sure, but always flat, always bounded. Spatial computing blows that up. Brand experiences can now exist in volumetric space — floating windows, wrapping interfaces, content that responds to gaze and gesture.\n\nThis isn't just a technical shift. It's conceptual. The design principles that govern flat interfaces — grids, typographic hierarchy, touch targets — need rethinking when the user *is* the viewport.\n\n## What to Do Right Now\n\n**Audit your assets.** Do your brand elements work in 3D? Logos designed for flat screens might need volumetric variants. Color systems built for backlit displays might need adjustment for mixed reality. Use AI mood board tools to quickly explore how existing assets translate to spatial contexts before investing in production.\n\n**Think presence, not presentation.** In spatial computing, a brand isn't just *seen* — it's *inhabited*. The question shifts from \"how does this look?\" to \"how does this feel to be around?\" It's closer to environmental design than screen design.\n\n**Prototype early.** The gap between flat mockups and immersive reality is huge. AI-assisted prototyping tools let you test spatial concepts and iterate on brand presence before committing to expensive volumetric production.\n\n## Why This Matters\n\nSpatial computing is the largest new design surface since mobile. The teams that explore it before it's obvious will be the ones that define the medium. The work is early, the tools are immature, and the audience is small. But the opportunity is immense — and first movers will set the standard.","category":"Technology","author":"Julian Vane","authorRole":"Principal Strategy","publishedAt":"Aug 3, 2025","readingTime":"5 min read","heroImage":"/images/case-studies/lamar-2.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":16,"searchTags":null},{"id":36,"slug":"sustainable-design-digital","title":"Sustainable Design Isn't Just Physical: The Carbon Cost of Digital Products","excerpt":"Every website has a carbon footprint. Every animation consumes energy. The design industry has a responsibility to consider the environmental impact of digital decisions.","body":"When people talk about sustainable design, they usually mean packaging and materials. But digital products have environmental costs too, and they're growing fast.\n\nThe internet accounts for roughly 3.7% of global carbon emissions — about the same as the airline industry. Every page load, image render, and animation burns energy. For a single site, it's negligible. For a site with millions of visitors? It adds up.\n\n## What Designers Can Actually Do\n\n**Optimize images.** They're the single biggest contributor to page weight. Modern formats (WebP, AVIF), proper compression, and responsive sizing can cut image payload by 50-80%.\n\n**Question every animation.** CSS animations beat JS animations for efficiency. Simple transitions beat particle effects. Infinite loops burn energy continuously. Every animation should earn its computational cost.\n\n**Design for efficiency.** Dark mode saves real energy on OLED screens. Efficient layouts reduce rendering costs. Lazy loading defers resource consumption until it's needed.\n\n**Use AI deliberately.** AI can actually support sustainable practices — fewer copy iterations, less full-production design rounds, quicker mood boarding. The key is using AI to be more deliberate, not more prolific.\n\n## The Point\n\nSustainable digital design isn't about sacrifice. It's about intention. And intention is what good design has always been about.","category":"Industry","author":"Sana Adler","authorRole":"UX Research Lead","publishedAt":"Jul 15, 2025","readingTime":"5 min read","heroImage":"/images/case-studies/merck-1.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":17,"searchTags":null},{"id":654,"slug":"choosing-design-agency-ai-company","title":"How to Choose the Right Design Agency for Your AI Company","excerpt":"Most design agencies weren't built for the AI era. Here's how to find one that actually gets it — and avoid the ones that'll waste your time and money.","body":"If you're building an AI product, you already know design isn't just about making things look nice. It's about building interfaces people trust, workflows that feel effortless, and experiences that actually show off what your AI can do.\n\nHere's the thing: most design agencies weren't built for this.\n\nThey're still running on traditional playbooks with teams that don't really understand what it means to design *for* AI. You need a partner who gets autonomous agents, explainability, human-AI collaboration, and all the ethical stuff that comes with the territory.\n\nThe stakes are real. Mediocre design tanks user adoption. Great design becomes your competitive edge.\n\n![Designing for AI products requires deep technical understanding](/images/case-studies/brainfm-2.png)\n\n## The Problems AI Companies Keep Running Into\n\n**Agencies aren't moving fast enough.** Most are still catching up. They might be experimenting with AI tools in their own workflows, but they haven't baked AI-native thinking into how they actually design. They'll approach your project with old-school methods that don't account for autonomous agent behavior, explainability, trust-building, or handling uncertainty and edge cases. You need an agency that's already designing *for* AI — not just *with* AI tools.\n\n**Tool chaos is real.** Your AI company runs on a specific stack. Your design agency uses something totally different. Getting those to play nice? That's where things fall apart. Agencies without interoperable workflows will slow you down. You'll burn weeks integrating Figma files with your dev pipeline or dealing with handoffs that create more friction than progress. The best agencies have already solved this with shared design systems and transparent pipelines.\n\n**Nobody wants to talk about ethics.** Here's what most agencies won't bring up: they don't have formal frameworks for responsible AI design. That's a massive red flag. When you're building AI products, you need to think about bias in interfaces, data privacy in design decisions, explainability, and accountability. Agencies without these frameworks are setting you up for legal, ethical, or reputational headaches down the road.\n\n![Product design that considers the full user journey](/images/case-studies/wonder-3.png)\n\n**Speed and cost expectations are misaligned.** AI companies move fast. You iterate quickly, test hypotheses, and pivot based on what you learn. Traditional agencies work on longer timelines with bigger budgets. They're used to retainers and lengthy discovery phases. What you need is flexibility, speed, and pricing that ties to outcomes. When an agency can't match your pace, the partnership is dead before it starts.\n\n## What to Actually Look For\n\n## AI Innovation & Technical Chops\n\nThis is non-negotiable. Your agency needs real AI expertise, not just buzzword fluency.\n\nLook for evidence of AI tool adoption in their own workflows, understanding of AI agent design patterns, experience designing for explainability and transparency, and familiarity with no-code AI agents and emerging trust-building interfaces.\n\nHow to gut-check it: ask them to explain how they'd design an interface for an autonomous AI agent. If they fumble, that tells you everything. Request examples of how they've used AI in their process. Check for blog posts or thought leadership. See if they have dedicated AI/ML designers.\n\n> The right agency doesn't just use AI tools — they understand what it means to design for AI-native experiences.\n\n**Red flags:** They talk about AI but can't name specific design challenges. Their portfolio has zero AI projects. They treat AI as a trend instead of a fundamental shift in how products work.\n\n![Deep technical understanding meets design craft](/images/case-studies/tavus-2.png)\n\n## Portfolio That Proves It\n\nYou need proof, not promises.\n\nLook for diverse, high-caliber projects with measurable impact. Case studies in AI, SaaS, or complex product design. Evidence of work in high-stakes industries like fintech, healthcare, or enterprise. Projects that show iteration, testing, and refinement — not just pretty screenshots. Clear metrics on outcomes like user adoption, engagement, and conversion.\n\nAsk for 3-5 case studies relevant to your industry. Get client references you can actually call. Look for depth — did they just make it look good, or did they solve real problems?\n\n**Red flags:** Portfolio is mostly branding or marketing work. Case studies lack specific metrics. They can't articulate the problems they solved. References are vague or conveniently unavailable.\n\n## Governance & Ethical Frameworks\n\nThis is what separates the serious agencies from the pretenders.\n\nYou want to see formal processes for bias auditing, data privacy protocols in design, explainability frameworks, human-in-the-loop practices, and compliance knowledge around GDPR, the AI Act, and industry-specific regulations.\n\n![Ethical AI design requires intentional frameworks](/images/case-studies/keycare-3.png)\n\nAsk them to walk through their governance process. Request documentation. Ask what happens if their AI design creates unintended consequences. If they look confused by the question, keep shopping.\n\n**Red flags:** They haven't thought about governance. No formal processes. Ethics is a checkbox they tick, not something they've internalized.\n\n## The Team Behind the Brand\n\nYou're hiring people, not a logo.\n\nMeet the actual team that will work on your project — not just the account managers. Look for AI/ML specialists or designers with real AI experience, product designers with deep technical knowledge, researchers who understand how people behave with AI, and engineers who bridge the gap between design and development.\n\n**Red flags:** You only ever meet sales people. High turnover or junior team members on key roles. Nobody on the team has actually shipped an AI product.\n\n## Process & How They Work With You\n\nHow they work matters just as much as what they produce.\n\nLook for transparent, iterative design processes with regular feedback loops. Collaborative tools that plug into your stack. Clear communication rhythms — weekly syncs, design reviews, async updates. Flexibility to adapt when things change (and they will). Outcome-based pricing that aligns incentives.\n\n![Collaborative design process in action](/images/case-studies/fb-3-1.png)\n\n**Red flags:** Rigid waterfall processes. Radio silence between milestones. Tools that don't talk to yours. Unwillingness to have an honest conversation about pricing.\n\n## The Hiring Playbook\n\n**Build your shortlist.** Find 5-10 agencies that specialize in AI, SaaS, or product design. Look for relevant case studies and teams actively writing about AI design topics.\n\n**Send a real brief.** Include your company background, your AI product, specific design challenges, timeline, budget range, and questions about their AI expertise and governance approach.\n\n**Have real conversations.** Talk to your top 3-5. Assess whether they actually understand your product, whether their communication style works for you, and whether you'd genuinely enjoy the collaboration.\n\n**Run a pilot.** Ask your top choice to audit your current design, propose solutions, and create a small exploration or prototype. This is the single best way to evaluate fit before committing.\n\n**Decide with your eyes open.** Compare finalists on the quality of their pilot work, clarity of their thinking, team chemistry, pricing model, and what their past clients actually say about them.\n\n![Strategic thinking meets execution](/images/case-studies/wonder-5.png)\n\n## The Stuff That Should Make You Walk Away\n\nThey don't ask questions about your AI product. If they're not curious, they won't design for it well.\n\nThey promise fast turnarounds without any discovery phase. Good design takes time — anyone who says otherwise is cutting corners.\n\nThey can't explain their design philosophy. If they don't know *why* they make the decisions they make, they're not thinking deeply enough.\n\nThey have no AI experience. It's 2026. If they haven't worked on AI products by now, they're behind.\n\nThey dodge conversations about pricing. Transparency matters — especially when you're about to spend a lot of money.\n\nThey have high client turnover. If clients don't stick around, there's usually a reason.\n\nThey treat ethics as optional. If bias, privacy, and explainability aren't on their radar, they're not ready for AI work.\n\n## Finding the Right Fit\n\nChoosing a design agency for your AI company comes down to finding a partner who understands AI at a real level — not just the hype, but the actual challenges of designing for autonomous systems, explainability, and trust. Someone who moves at your pace, thinks about ethics without being asked, has a track record of shipping AI products, and genuinely collaborates with your team instead of operating in a silo.\n\nThe right agency becomes an extension of your team. They challenge your assumptions, push your thinking, and help you build products people actually love using.\n\nTake your time with this. Ask the hard questions. Call the references. Do the pilot project. The upfront investment in finding the right partner pays back tenfold in product quality and speed.","category":"Strategy","author":"Unfold Team","authorRole":"Design Agency","publishedAt":"Feb 20, 2026","readingTime":"7 min read","heroImage":"/images/case-studies/wonder-2.png","featured":false,"displayOrder":18,"searchTags":null}]